condition_name: Alveolar Osteitis (Dry Socket)
condition_slug: alveolar-osteitis-dry-socket
source: NSTG 2022
introduction: The most frequent painful complication of extractions. Caused by destruction of the clot that normally fills the socket
clinical_features[1]:
  - type: Clinical Features of Alveolar Osteitis
    features[4]: Pain delayed for few days up to a week after extraction,"Deep seated, throbbing pain",Mucosa around socket is red and tender,No clot in socket - bare whitish alveolar bone exposed
investigations[0]:
treatment:
  goals[3]: Alleviate pain and suffering of the patient,Optimize condition for healing and epitheliasation of the extraction socket,Keep open socket clean and protect exposed bone
  non_drug[3]: Irrigate with mild warm saline and antiseptic,Fill with an obtundant dressing containing some non-irritant antiseptic,Warm saline mouth rinse
  drug[3]: "Local anaesthesia: lidocaine 2% (1 in 80,000)","Co-amoxiclav: 250/125 mg orally every 8 hours for 5 days (dose doubled in severe infections)",10 mL of 2% chlorhexidine gluconate solution for mouth wash three times daily
  adverse_reactions_and_cautions[0]:
  supportive_measures[0]:
differential_diagnoses[1]: Osteomyelitis
complications[1]: Osteomyelitis
prevention[3]: Minimal trauma during extractions,"Immediately after extraction, squeeze socket edges firmly together and hold for a few minutes till clot has formed","Antibiotics if patients have had irradiation, or have Paget's disease"
other_investigations: null
definitive_treatment: null
prognosis: null